          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            In the section
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondences"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondences
            we had seen how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            is captured by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoids"} smooth groupoids
            slices over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)
          %p
            We consider this now as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Poisson-Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Poisson-Chern-Simons theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"} smooth infinity-groupoids
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)
            and discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            from the point of view of the holographic/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} motivic quantization
            of this system.
          %p
            That is we consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SG
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SG(X,\pi)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\pi)
            as equipped with its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+n-bundle"} prequantum line 2-bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(X,\pi) \to \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-module+bundle"} (infinity,1)-module bundle
            by composing with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi GL
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi KU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)\to B GL_1(KU)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+K-theory"} fiber integration in K-theory
            and show how this reproduces, on the boundary, traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            such as in particular the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
          .maruku-equation
